    "title": "A parity law for prime k-tuple representations",
    "description": "For an integer N, the ordered representations N = p + (N - p) with both summands prime are permuted by the reflection p -> N - p, whose only fixed point is the diagonal p = N/2. This forces the number of such representations to be odd precisely when N is even and N/2 is prime. Extend this parity phenomenon to representations of N as an ordered sum of k primes for k >= 3, where the symmetric group S_k acts on the coordinates and the fixed points are the 'balanced' tuples. Determine, for each k, an exact congruence for the representation count modulo the order of the relevant stabilizer subgroups, expressed purely in terms of which balanced sub-configurations of N are simultaneously prime. Test the resulting congruences against direct enumeration for small N and k.",
